1. Overview
Government supply chains span thousands of suppliers, sub-tier manufacturers, logistics corridors, and partner agencies. Disruptions propagate fast: a single facility going offline — a semiconductor fab, a reagent manufacturer, a cold-chain packager — can halt production across multiple critical programs within days. Most agencies still rely on manual tracking, fragmented spreadsheets, and reactive crisis response.

This guide walks through building a complete supply chain resilience platform on Snowflake, modeled on the reference scenario of Pacific Shield Logistics Command, a fictitious federal coordination unit responsible for critical supply chain visibility across defense, energy, and medical sectors. Everything runs inside Snowflake — no data leaves the platform.

Why Now?
Supply chain complexity is growing. Global sourcing, sole-source dependencies, and just-in-time logistics have increased fragility. Agencies need continuous visibility, not quarterly snapshots.

AI enables predictive resilience. CoCo brings Cortex AI and Snowpark ML into the secure SnowGov perimeter so agencies can model disruption scenarios and flag at-risk suppliers without moving sensitive sourcing data outside the platform.

Conversational access removes the SQL bottleneck. CoWork lets planners ask questions like "which medical supply chains have the highest concentration risk?" in plain English and get a governed, prioritized answer — no dashboard-building required.

Cross-agency coordination is a mandate. Executive orders require federal agencies to share supply chain intelligence. Governed, zero-copy data sharing makes this possible without duplicating data.

