3. Unified Operating Picture (Governed Views)
The first thing a planner needs is one place to see concentration risk, delays, and quality signal together — not three separate systems.

CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW VW_UNIFIED_OPERATING_PICTURE AS
WITH bom_agg AS (
SELECT SUPPLIER_ID,
COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CRITICALITY = 'HIGH' THEN PROGRAM_ID END) AS critical_programs
FROM BILL_OF_MATERIALS GROUP BY SUPPLIER_ID
),
logistics_agg AS (
SELECT SUPPLIER_ID,
COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN STATUS IN ('DELAYED','AT_RISK') THEN SHIPMENT_ID END) AS delayed_shipments
FROM LOGISTICS_EVENTS GROUP BY SUPPLIER_ID
),
quality_agg AS (
SELECT SUPPLIER_ID,
COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN INCIDENT_DATE >= DATEADD(day, -90, CURRENT_DATE()) THEN INCIDENT_ID END) AS incidents_90d
FROM QUALITY_INCIDENTS GROUP BY SUPPLIER_ID
)
SELECT s.SUPPLIER_ID, s.SUPPLIER_NAME, s.SECTOR, s.TIER, s.IS_SINGLE_SOURCE, s.RISK_SCORE,
CASE WHEN s.IS_SINGLE_SOURCE THEN COALESCE(b.critical_programs, 0) ELSE 0 END AS single_source_critical_programs,
COALESCE(l.delayed_shipments, 0) AS delayed_or_at_risk_shipments,
COALESCE(q.incidents_90d, 0) AS quality_incidents_last_90_days
FROM SUPPLIERS s
LEFT JOIN bom_agg b ON b.SUPPLIER_ID = s.SUPPLIER_ID
LEFT JOIN logistics_agg l ON l.SUPPLIER_ID = s.SUPPLIER_ID
LEFT JOIN quality_agg q ON q.SUPPLIER_ID = s.SUPPLIER_ID;

Important: aggregate each fact table (bom_agg, logistics_agg, quality_agg) in its own CTE before joining. Joining BILL_OF_MATERIALS, LOGISTICS_EVENTS, and QUALITY_INCIDENTS to SUPPLIERS all at once and then aggregating causes a fan-out — contract values and counts get silently multiplied by however many rows exist in the other joined tables. This is the single most common correctness bug in supply-chain-style multi-fact reporting.
Production note: for continuous refresh as new shipments, risk feeds, and quality incidents land, convert this view to a Dynamic Table with TARGET_LAG matched to your data arrival cadence:
CREATE OR REPLACE DYNAMIC TABLE DT_UNIFIED_OPERATING_PICTURE
TARGET_LAG = '1 hour'
WAREHOUSE = NEXUS_WH
AS
SELECT * FROM VW_UNIFIED_OPERATING_PICTURE; -- or inline the CTEs directly

4. Disruption Classification (Cortex AI)
This is the centerpiece of the platform: trace what happens when a specific facility — not a whole supplier, a specific manufacturing node — goes offline for 30 days.

CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW VW_DISRUPTION_SCENARIO AS
SELECT
b.FACILITY_ID, b.FACILITY_COUNTRY, b.PROGRAM_ID, p.PROGRAM_NAME, p.SECTOR, p.AGENCY,
b.COMPONENT_NAME, s.SUPPLIER_NAME AS PRIMARY_SUPPLIER, b.LEAD_TIME_DAYS AS NORMAL_LEAD_TIME_DAYS,
alt.SUPPLIER_NAME AS ALTERNATIVE_SUPPLIER, alt.COUNTRY AS ALTERNATIVE_SUPPLIER_COUNTRY,
b.ALT_LEAD_TIME_DAYS, b.ALT_CAPACITY_PCT,
(b.ALT_LEAD_TIME_DAYS - b.LEAD_TIME_DAYS) AS ESTIMATED_PROGRAM_DELAY_DAYS
FROM BILL_OF_MATERIALS b
JOIN PROGRAMS p ON p.PROGRAM_ID = b.PROGRAM_ID
JOIN SUPPLIERS s ON s.SUPPLIER_ID = b.SUPPLIER_ID
LEFT JOIN SUPPLIERS alt ON alt.SUPPLIER_ID = b.ALT_SUPPLIER_ID
ORDER BY ESTIMATED_PROGRAM_DELAY_DAYS DESC;

-- Run the scenario for a specific facility, e.g. a semiconductor fab:

SELECT * FROM VW_DISRUPTION_SCENARIO WHERE FACILITY_ID = 'TW-FAB-01';

In our reference build, filtering this view for a single Taiwan semiconductor fab surfaced 14 downstream programs affected across defense, energy, and medical sectors — with per-program delay estimates and ranked alternative suppliers — from one query, in seconds. That number used to take a coordination unit three weeks to piece together manually across spreadsheets.

Explaining the disruption in plain language (Cortex AI)
Pair the structural model above with COMPLETE to turn a raw disruption event or quality incident into an analyst-facing explanation:

SELECT
FEED_ID, REGION, RISK_TYPE, DESCRIPTION,
SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX.COMPLETE(
'claude-4-sonnet',
'You are a supply chain risk analyst for a government agency. Given this risk feed entry, '
|| 'explain in 2-3 sentences the likely downstream impact and recommended immediate action. '
|| 'Entry: ' || DESCRIPTION || ' | Region: ' || REGION || ' | Risk type: ' || RISK_TYPE
) AS ANALYST_EXPLANATION
FROM MARKETPLACE_RISK_FEED
WHERE RISK_SCORE >= 65;
CoCo note: in SnowGov regions, this COMPLETE call and the Snowpark ML model in Section 5 both execute inside the FedRAMP-authorized perimeter via CoCo — sensitive sourcing data is never sent to an external model endpoint.

This scenario view is also what powers the live, interactive simulator in the Streamlit app (Section 7) — a planner picks any facility from a dropdown and this exact query reruns on the spot.

5. Predictive Scoring with ML in Snowpark
Rather than a pure-SQL model object, this section builds the supplier-failure-risk model as a Snowpark ML pipeline — the pattern you'd actually use once you want feature engineering, experimentation, and model versioning beyond what a single SQL statement supports.

from snowflake.snowpark import Session
from snowflake.snowpark.functions import col
from snowflake.ml.modeling.ensemble import GradientBoostingClassifier
from snowflake.ml.modeling.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from snowflake.ml.registry import Registry

session = Session.builder.getOrCreate()

# 1. Feature engineering, pushed down to Snowflake compute
features_df = session.table("VW_UNIFIED_OPERATING_PICTURE").select(
"SUPPLIER_ID",
"RISK_SCORE",
"SINGLE_SOURCE_CRITICAL_PROGRAMS",
"DELAYED_OR_AT_RISK_SHIPMENTS",
"QUALITY_INCIDENTS_LAST_90_DAYS",
col("TIER").cast("float"),
)

# 2. Join to historical outcome labels (supplier experienced a critical
# failure -- contract termination, force majeure, insolvency -- within 90 days)
training_df = features_df.join(
session.table("SUPPLIER_FAILURE_LABELS"), on="SUPPLIER_ID"
)

# 3. Scale + train, using Snowpark ML's scikit-learn-compatible API --
# runs as a stored procedure inside Snowflake, no data egress
scaler = StandardScaler(input_cols=["RISK_SCORE", "DELAYED_OR_AT_RISK_SHIPMENTS"],
output_cols=["RISK_SCORE_SCALED", "DELAYS_SCALED"])
scaled_df = scaler.fit(training_df).transform(training_df)

model = GradientBoostingClassifier(
input_cols=["RISK_SCORE_SCALED", "SINGLE_SOURCE_CRITICAL_PROGRAMS",
"DELAYS_SCALED", "QUALITY_INCIDENTS_LAST_90_DAYS", "TIER"],
label_cols=["FAILED_WITHIN_90D"],
output_cols=["PREDICTED_FAILURE_RISK"],
)
model.fit(scaled_df)

# 4. Register the model for governed, versioned reuse
registry = Registry(session=session)
registry.log_model(
model, model_name="SUPPLIER_FAILURE_MODEL", version_name="v1",
comment="Gradient boosting classifier for 90-day supplier failure risk",
)

# 5. Batch score every supplier and write results back to a table
scored_df = model.predict(scaled_df)
scored_df.write.save_as_table("SUPPLIER_FAILURE_PREDICTIONS", mode="overwrite")
This runs as a Snowpark stored procedure or from a Notebook — either way, training and inference stay inside Snowflake's compute, and the registered model is reusable, versioned, and auditable through the Model Registry rather than being a one-off SQL object.

Why Snowpark over the SQL-native ML functions here: the SQL-native SNOWFLAKE.ML.CLASSIFICATION/ANOMALY_DETECTION objects are great for a fast first pass, but once you need custom feature engineering, hyperparameter tuning, or to swap in a different algorithm, Snowpark ML's scikit-learn-compatible API gives you that flexibility while keeping everything in Snowflake's governed compute.
6. Investigation Agent (Cortex Agent + CoWork)
One agent orchestrates the semantic view so planners can ask natural-language questions across suppliers, programs, procurement, logistics, and risk — no SQL required.

Step 1: Create the semantic view
CREATE OR REPLACE SEMANTIC VIEW PACIFIC_SHIELD_SUPPLY_CHAIN_SV
TABLES (
SUPPLIERS PRIMARY KEY (SUPPLIER_ID),
PROGRAMS PRIMARY KEY (PROGRAM_ID),
BILL_OF_MATERIALS PRIMARY KEY (BOM_ID)
)
RELATIONSHIPS (
BOM_TO_SUPPLIERS AS BILL_OF_MATERIALS (SUPPLIER_ID) REFERENCES SUPPLIERS,
BOM_TO_PROGRAMS AS BILL_OF_MATERIALS (PROGRAM_ID) REFERENCES PROGRAMS
)
FACTS ( SUPPLIERS.RISK_SCORE_FACT AS RISK_SCORE )
DIMENSIONS (
SUPPLIERS.SUPPLIER_NAME AS SUPPLIER_NAME,
SUPPLIERS.IS_SINGLE_SOURCE AS IS_SINGLE_SOURCE,
PROGRAMS.PROGRAM_SECTOR AS SECTOR
)
METRICS (
SUPPLIERS.SINGLE_SOURCE_SUPPLIER_COUNT AS COUNT(CASE WHEN SUPPLIERS.IS_SINGLE_SOURCE THEN SUPPLIERS.SUPPLIER_ID END),
CONCENTRATION_RISK_PCT AS 100.0 * SUPPLIERS.SINGLE_SOURCE_SUPPLIER_COUNT / SUPPLIERS.SUPPLIER_COUNT
);

Step 2: Create the agent
Cortex Agents are created with CREATE AGENT ... FROM SPECIFICATION, not a TOOLS = (...) list — the specification is JSON, and each tool is wired to its resource (a semantic view, a search service, or a custom function):

CREATE OR REPLACE AGENT PACIFIC_SHIELD_ANALYST
WITH PROFILE='{"display_name": "Pacific Shield Supply Chain Analyst"}'
FROM SPECIFICATION $$
{
"models": { "orchestration": "auto" },
"instructions": {
"response": "You are the Pacific Shield Logistics Command supply chain analyst. Answer questions about suppliers, programs, procurement, logistics, and concentration risk clearly and concisely, citing specific numbers from the data.",
"orchestration": "Use the supply chain analyst tool for any question about suppliers, programs, procurement, logistics, or risk.",
"sample_questions": [
{"question": "Which tier-two suppliers are single-sourced for critical components?"},
{"question": "Which medical supply chains have the highest concentration risk?"}
]
},
"tools": [
{"tool_spec": {"type": "cortex_analyst_text_to_sql", "name": "supply_chain_analyst",
"description": "Answers questions about suppliers, programs, procurement, logistics, and risk."}}
],
"tool_resources": {
"supply_chain_analyst": {"semantic_view": "PACIFIC_SHIELD_DEMO.SUPPLY_CHAIN.PACIFIC_SHIELD_SUPPLY_CHAIN_SV"}
}
}
$$;
Add a cortex_search tool to the tools array (with a matching entry in tool_resources) if you later extend the platform with Cortex Search over unstructured content such as audit documents or coordination-call transcripts.

Step 3: Surface it in CoWork
The agent appears automatically in CoWork (https://ai.snowflake.com/<org>/<account>) once USAGE is granted:

GRANT USAGE ON AGENT PACIFIC_SHIELD_ANALYST TO ROLE <analyst_role>;
Planners can then ask, in plain English, with no SQL:

"Which medical supply chains have the highest concentration risk?"

"Which tier-two suppliers are single-sourced for critical components?"

"What happens if this facility goes offline for 30 days?"

The agent routes each question to the right tool automatically and returns a governed, explainable answer.

